  [1mThe fork:[0m nothing in this app currently has a userId to pass. The form collects only username and password
  ([38;5;153mindex.html:9-10[39m), and [38;5;153mlogin[39m is a stub that returns [38;5;153m{ success: true, user: username }[39m. So "add a userId parameter"
  needs a source for that value, and the three plausible sources produce different interfaces.

  - [1mClient-generated attempt id[0m — the handler mints an id (e.g. [38;5;153mcrypto.randomUUID()[39m) per login attempt and passes it in.
    Buys you a real correlation id for tracking each attempt end-to-end, costs one line in the handler, and is the only
    option that works today with no server. It does [3mnot[0m identify the person — it identifies the attempt.
  - [1mCaller-supplied, optional third param[0m — [38;5;153mlogin(username, password, userId)[39m with [38;5;153muserId[39m defaulting to [38;5;153mnull[39m. Buys you
    the signature you asked for with the existing caller still working unchanged. Costs: the handler has nothing real to
    pass, so it stays [38;5;153mnull[39m until something upstream produces one — the parameter is scaffolding for later.
  - [1muserId as a return value, not a parameter[0m — the server identifies the user after authenticating, so [38;5;153mlogin[39m returns [38;5;153m{
[39m    [38;5;153msuccess, user, userId }[39m and the handler logs it. Buys you the actual "who logged in" fact. Costs: it's not the
    change you asked for, and the stub would fake the id until there's a real API.

  Cheap to change later: the client-generated id and the logging. Not cheap: the parameter position and whether [38;5;153muserId[39m
  is an input or an output — once a real API is wired to [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m, callers depend on that shape.

  [1mMy recommendation: the optional third param.[0m It's what you asked for, it keeps the one existing caller working, and it
  doesn't invent a tracking scheme you didn't ask for. But I want to flag that it will hold [38;5;153mnull[39m in the only call site
  until something supplies a userId — if the goal is genuinely "track who logged in" and you want that working now, the
  client-generated attempt id is the option that actually delivers it.
